 "substitutedBy": null,
 "summary": "Requires fees charged by municipal industrial development agencies be the same as those charged by the county's industrial development agency.",
 "title": "Relates to fees charged by municipal industrial development agencies",
 "vetoMessages": {
  "items": [
   {
    "billId": {
     "basePrintNo": "A795",
     "basePrintNoStr": "A795-2021",
     "printNo": "A795",
     "session": 2021,
     "version": ""
    },
    "billPage": 0,
    "chapter": 0,
    "lineEnd": 0,
    "lineStart": 0,
    "memoText": " \n                          VETO MESSAGE - No. 68\n \nTO THE ASSEMBLY:\n \nI am returning herewith, without my approval, the following bill:\n \nAssembly Bill Number 795, entitled:\n \n    \"AN  ACT  to  amend  the  general municipal law, in relation to fees\n      charged by municipal industrial development agencies\"\n \n    NOT APPROVED\n \n  This well intentioned bill would amend the General  Municipal  Law  by\nadding  a  new subdivision to require each municipal industrial develop-\nment agency or authority (IDA) located entirely within a county that has\nits own county IDA to charge the same fees as the county IDA. This  bill\nattempts to address a real problem that the Legislature has identified -\nusing  municipal  tax  expenditures  to  lure  a project from one IDA to\nanother within a county for the purposes of receiving  state  and  local\ntax breaks.\n \n  A  recent  State  Senate  report  on public authorities and industrial\ndevelopment agencies found growth of public authority  debt,  inadequate\nstatutory  oversight,  and insufficient transparency. The report singled\nout an Orange County warehouse expansion project seeking Town  of  Mont-\ngomery IDA incentives, after the application for the incentive was ques-\ntioned  by  the Orange County IDA. The report identified this project as\nan example of how IDA financial incentives can be abused to  the  detri-\nment of local taxpayers.\n \n  The  Senate Committee on Investigations' evidence show how the project\nseeking an IDA's tax expenditures were  shopped  within  a  county.  The\nMedline  Industries  warehouse  project  seeking  Town of Montgomery IDA\nincentives was singled out as one that reveals the  \"unethical,  if  not\nillegal,  behavior\" this bill would seek to address. It is as an example\nof how financial incentives can be abused  to  the  detriment  of  local\ntaxpayers.  Ultimately,  because of public exposure, the application for\nthis project was ultimately withdrawn.\n \n  Today IDAs work together inter-regionally on projects,  including  the\nRegional Economic Development Council projects, supported by their larg-\ner  economies.  Most fees are within a quarter percent of each other and\nfactors such as geography, time to market, and workforce are key reasons\nwhy business chooses to expand or locate  within  a  certain  community.\nWhile I support the intent of this bill to prevent intra-county competi-\ntion  among  IDAs  for  projects,  it  misses the mark. The hands of the\nentire IDA system should not be tied because of one bad actor's example.\nThe justification assumes that different  fee  structures  give  project\napplicants  an incentive to \"shop around\", but this is not indicative of\nthe hundreds of projects that are approved by IDAs across the state.  In\nperhaps limited instances, project applicants may have sought out an IDA\nfor  its  lower  fees.  However, the one-size-fits-all approach taken by\nthis bill would disadvantage municipal IDAs across the  State  in  their\nefforts to achieve their specific economic development goals.\n \n  IDAs play a critical role in ensuring the economic health and vitality\nof  New  York's municipalities and counties. Municipal as well as county\n \nIDAs are better able to serve their constituents when  they  retain  the\nflexibility  to  tailor their own policies and activities in response to\nthe economic and fiscal concerns and needs of their  local  communities.\nThis  bill  would  undermine  the autonomy of municipal IDAs by limiting\ntheir ability to implement fee structures that align with  their  objec-\ntives, including supporting operations as well as attracting investment.\n \n  For these reasons, I am constrained to veto this bill.\n \n  The bill is disapproved.                       (signed) KATHY HOCHUL\n \n                               __________\n",
